{
  "id": "T18_61_two_expectations_are_compared_under_their_own_interpretations",
  "family": "T18",
  "rules": [
    "7.2.4",
    "7.1.5",
    "7.3.8",
    "9.4.2",
    "7.6.12",
    "7.6.5",
    "7.6.6",
    "9.5.1",
    "9.5.2",
    "9.6.1",
    "12.20"
  ],
  "description": "Three pairs of expectations on A-601 and A-602 are in force and not fulfilled. Door 106's pair requires \"90 min\" in a context naming the fixture fire-rating interpretation and \"1.5 h\" in a context naming none. Door 116's pair requires \"90 min\" and \"1.5 h\", and Door 117's \"90 min\" and \"60 min\", each in contexts that both name the fire-rating interpretation. A frame naming no interpretation compares each side under its own. Door 116's pair is never a conflict, before or after any normalization, and Door 117's always is, carrying the sides' interpretations while they differ from the read's. Door 106's pair is ambiguous on Tuesday, matched by the Wednesday normalization of the project on Thursday, and ambiguous again after a Friday normalization to literal comparison; a frame naming the fire-rating interpretation compares both sides under it and finds no conflict. A story recorded for Door 116's pair has no period: its end act has cause interpretation, cites the two expectations and has no time or since, and it reads ended at every cutoff. The story recorded for Door 106's pair reports the pair of interpretations and an ambiguous disagreement where they differ, and its aligned sides, matched by the normalization, where they agree.",
  "note": "7.2.4: \"A conflict story whose sides are two expectations instead compares each side under that expectation's own interpretation at the instant (7.6.13). When the two are the same, as when both contexts name one interpretation or neither names any, that one is the story's interpretation. When they differ, the story's interpretation is the pair: the sides are equal when they are equal under both, unequal when they are unequal under both, and otherwise their comparison is ambiguous (4.5.3), listing the two expectations.\" 9.4.2: pairs of expectations \"that require unequal values under the frame's interpretation or, where it names none, under their own interpretations at the cutoff\". 7.6.12: \"for a conflict story between two expectations whose contexts both name an interpretation, and whose sides, at that instant, are equal under those interpretations (7.2.4, 7.6.13), cause `interpretation`, with the two expectations as its record (7.6.6).\" README section 5, `conflicts`: an expectation pair's entry \"carries `interpretations`, the two sides' interpretation names (`null` for literal comparison) in side order, when either differs from the read's `interpretation`\". Door 106's story transitions, which take the ambiguous form of 7.6.6, and the normalizations' maintenance of it are not pinned; whether Door 116's pair is listed as matched after the Wednesday normalization, whose interpretation its contexts already name, is not pinned either.",
